# Generated-artifact merge conflicts (PROC-12): a conflict on one of
# these files means two branches each hold a stale or independently
# regenerated copy, never a real semantic disagreement worth a human's
# time. See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-proc-12-regenerate-on-conflict-design.md.

# Tier A — append-only lists (git's builtin union driver; no setup step,
# no risk of dropping either side's addition, at most a cosmetic
# ordering difference between the two sides' new lines).
#
# CAVEAT: union keeps BOTH sides' lines, which is right for an append and
# WRONG for a rewrite — it duplicates the entry instead of resolving the
# edit. A campaign that rewrites existing lines here (e.g. The Shelf-Mark
# compacting 43 registry rows) must override this for its own merge:
#   echo 'book/src/frontier/idea-registry.md merge' > .git/info/attributes
# (.git/info/attributes outranks this file; delete it after the merge.)
# See Addendum A of the PROC-12 spec below.
book/src/SUMMARY.md merge=union
book/src/frontier/idea-registry.md merge=union
# docs/timings.md is the most-appended file in the repo — every census,
# rebaseline, ci run and (since gate timings landed) every gate adds a row,
# from whichever session and machine ran it. Rows are historical records that
# are never rewritten, so union's caveat above does not bite: there is no edit
# to resolve, only two sides' appends to keep. Missed when PROC-12 shipped.
docs/timings.md merge=union
# docs/retrospectives/README.md is the campaign index: one row per campaign,
# inserted into the themed section it belongs to. Two campaigns closing into the
# SAME section append adjacent lines and collide -- observed 2026-09-05 between
# campaign/the-zenith and campaign/the-tenon, whose only real conflict against
# each other was this file. The union caveat above was CHECKED against this
# file's history rather than assumed: of the last 20 commits touching it, 18 are
# a pure one-line insert and the two that rewrite a row (ad4908e55, 1b677c1c8)
# are each a campaign amending ITS OWN row on its own branch, which no other
# side touches. A cross-campaign rewrite -- the shape union gets wrong -- has not
# occurred here. If one ever does (a compaction pass over existing rows, as The
# Shelf-Mark did to the idea registry), override it for that merge exactly as
# the caveat above describes.
docs/retrospectives/README.md merge=union
docs/audits/campaign-reconciliation.tsv merge=union
# docs/audits/campaign-reconciliation.tsv is one row per campaign record,
# keyed by slug, appended at close. It became the single most common conflict
# in the queue: six campaigns bounced or collided on it in twelve hours on
# 2026-09-05/06 (the-lot, the-ken, the-warrant, the-culvert,
# codex/federation-next, the-hidage), every time on the tail, every time
# resolved by hand by unioning two appends.
#
# THE CAVEAT ABOVE DOES BITE HERE, UNLIKE docs/timings.md, AND THE NUMBER IS
# MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED: 7 of the last 30 commits to this file contain
# DELETIONS, so roughly a quarter rewrite a row instead of only appending. It
# is therefore the idea-registry.md case, not the timings.md case — union is
# taken knowing that edits happen.
#
# Why it is still right: union's bad case needs two branches editing THE SAME
# ROW, not merely both touching the file, and rows are keyed by campaign slug
# with each campaign editing its own. When it does go wrong the product has a
# DUPLICATE KEY, which cli/tests/suite/docs_consistency.rs rejects at gate
# time — a loud, immediate failure replacing a constant quiet one. The quiet
# one has already cost real rows: campaign/the-warp lost its two by having the
# conflict "resolved by regeneration" before the file was declared
# hand-authored in docs/generated-paths.txt.
#
# Reversible in one line. scripts/test-reconciliation-union.sh proves both
# directions against real git merges rather than asserting them.


# TIER B IS RETIRED (decision 0166). Six fully-re-derived documents used to
# route through `merge=hv-regenerate` (scripts/merge-regenerate.sh), which
# discarded both sides' text and reran the generator. The premise was right
# and the implementation could not deliver it: a merge driver runs BEFORE the
# merge product exists on disk — git's `ort` has not yet written the incoming
# files — so regeneration at that moment measures the wrong tree and emits
# ours' answer, missing theirs.
#
# It is not that the driver sometimes failed. Git invokes a driver only when
# BOTH sides changed the path, which is exactly when the two sources differ,
# which is exactly when the working tree is not the merge product. Every
# invocation was wrong; merges where these files came out right are merges
# where the driver never ran. Measured: the same two commits merged in
# opposite directions produced two clean, conflict-free, DIFFERENT results,
# one dropping six primitives and the other dropping an entire crate's row
# from a default-deny audit.
#
# These six paths now take an ordinary three-way merge, which is strictly
# better: it can at least conflict and make a human look. The mechanism that
# actually works is scripts/hooks/post-merge's advisory — run `make
# rebaseline` and diff the paths docs/generated-paths.txt declares — and it
# is what caught all three of the 2026-08-20 incidents.
#
# `cli/tests/suite/generated_paths.rs` fails if a `merge=hv-regenerate`
# attribute reappears here. Read decision 0166 before re-adding one.
